  "title": "William Harvey",
  "summary": "William Harvey (1578-1657), born at Folkestone in Kent, studied at Cambridge and at Padua, then the leading medical school in Europe. He became physician to St Bartholomew's Hospital in London and royal physician to James I and Charles I. In De Motu Cordis (1628), drawing on dissection, experiments on living animals and calculations of how much blood the heart moves, he showed that the heart is a muscular pump driving the blood round the body in a continuous circle, overturning the teaching of Galen.",
